          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which combination of findings defines aplastic anaemia?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which combination of findings defines aplastic anaemia?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pancytopenia with a hypercellular marrow containing megaloblasts</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This describes megaloblastic anaemia due to vitamin B12 or folate deficiency. Aplastic anaemia has a hypocellular marrow, not a hypercellular one.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pancytopenia with a hypocellular marrow showing no dysplasia, fibrosis, or infiltration</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Both criteria must be met simultaneously — pancytopenia in the blood AND a hypocellular marrow free of malignant cells, fibrosis, and infiltrative deposits.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Isolated anaemia with a hypocellular marrow and ring sideroblasts</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Ring sideroblasts indicate myelodysplastic syndrome. Aplastic anaemia involves all three cell lines (pancytopenia), not isolated anaemia, and the marrow must be free of dysplastic features.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pancytopenia with marrow fibrosis and tear-drop poikilocytes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Marrow fibrosis with tear-drop cells is the hallmark of myelofibrosis. In aplastic anaemia, fibrosis must specifically be absent from the marrow.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Aplastic anaemia is defined by pancytopenia combined with a hypocellular bone marrow. Three features must be absent: dysplasia or malignancy, fibrosis, and infiltration by granulomas or metastatic deposits."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Aplastic anaemia requires both pancytopenia and a hypocellular marrow, with the specific exclusion of dysplastic or malignant cells, fibrosis, and infiltrative deposits."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
