        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 322.625,
            "to": 332.625
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What is the dominant mechanism of marrow failure in most cases of aplastic anaem</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What is the dominant mechanism of marrow failure in most cases of aplastic anaemia?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Replacement of the marrow cavity by fibrotic tissue</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fibrotic replacement is the mechanism in myelofibrosis. In aplastic anaemia, the marrow space is structurally intact but emptied of haematopoietic cells.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Blast cell proliferation crowding out normal precursors</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Blast infiltration is the mechanism in leukaemia. The marrow in aplastic anaemia specifically lacks blast cells.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cytotoxic T-lymphocyte attack on haematopoietic stem cells</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Activated CTLs destroy stem cells through three pathways — Fas-mediated apoptosis, perforin-granzyme release, and IFN-γ/TNF-α secretion — accounting for roughly 80% of cases.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inherited telomerase mutations preventing stem-cell renewal</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Telomerase mutations cause dyskeratosis congenita, a rare inherited form. The dominant mechanism overall is immune-mediated destruction, not intrinsic stem-cell defects.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Approximately 80% of aplastic anaemia cases are driven by immune-mediated destruction. Activated cytotoxic T lymphocytes attack haematopoietic stem cells via Fas signalling, perforin/granzyme, and inhibitory cytokines."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! The immune-mediated pathway accounts for approximately 80% of cases, which is why immunosuppressive therapy with anti-thymocyte globulin and ciclosporin is effective."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
