          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which peripheral blood finding confirms that the bone marrow has stopped produci</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which peripheral blood finding confirms that the bone marrow has stopped producing new red blood cells in aplastic anaemia?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>A very low or absent reticulocyte count</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Reticulocytopenia proves the marrow is no longer releasing young red cells. It also distinguishes aplasia from haemolytic anaemia, where reticulocyte production rises as the marrow compensates.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Macrocytic red blood cells with elevated MCV</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Macrocytosis is characteristic of megaloblastic anaemia from B12 or folate deficiency. In aplastic anaemia, the red cells are normocytic and normochromic.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hypersegmented neutrophils on the blood film</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hypersegmented neutrophils indicate megaloblastic anaemia. They are specifically absent in aplastic anaemia, and their absence is itself a key diagnostic clue.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Tear-drop shaped poikilocytes on the smear</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Tear-drop cells (dacrocytes) are characteristic of myelofibrosis, where fibrosis distorts red cells as they squeeze out of the marrow. They are absent in aplastic anaemia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "A very low or absent reticulocyte count (reticulocytopenia) confirms the marrow is no longer producing new red cells. This distinguishes aplasia from haemolysis, where reticulocyte counts rise compensatorily."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Reticulocytopenia is the hallmark peripheral finding confirming marrow failure. In aplastic anaemia, the cells that remain look normal — it is their absence that is diagnostic."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
