          "label": "<p>For which purpose has bone marrow examination been largely replaced by a simpler</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>For which purpose has bone marrow examination been largely replaced by a simpler blood test?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Staging of lymphoma using the Ann Arbor system</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Lymphoma staging still requires bone marrow examination. The Ann Arbor system uses marrow biopsy to detect infiltration and assign the correct stage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Classification and cytogenetics of acute leukaemia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Acute leukaemia classification depends on marrow morphology, immunophenotyping, and cytogenetics — no peripheral blood test can replace these.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Monitoring engraftment after stem-cell transplantation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Post-transplant marrow examination remains essential to confirm that donor cells have engrafted and are producing blood cells normally.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Confirming iron deficiency using Prussian blue staining</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Historically, Prussian blue staining of marrow was the gold standard for assessing iron stores. Today, serum ferritin has largely replaced this invasive procedure for routine diagnosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Bone marrow Prussian blue staining for iron stores has been largely replaced by serum ferritin measurement. All other listed indications — leukaemia classification, lymphoma staging, and transplant monitoring — still require marrow examination."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Serum ferritin now serves as the primary method for assessing body iron stores, making bone marrow Prussian blue staining unnecessary for routine iron deficiency diagnosis."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
